# Userland Conventions

## Hard Boundary: Userland vs Kernel
- Userland code **NEVER** includes kernel headers (`kernel/include/`, `kernel/core/`, `kernel/arch/`)
- Userland code **NEVER** calls kernel functions directly (`kmalloc`, `printk`, `serial_puts`, etc.)
- The only kernel interface is **syscalls** via `userland/include/syscall.h`
- If you need something from the kernel in userland, it must go through a syscall wrapper in `userland/libc/`

## Directory Layout
```
userland/
├── include/
│   └── syscall.h       ← syscall numbers + raw asm wrappers (already exists — do not recreate)
├── libc/
│   ├── include/        ← public libc headers (stdio.h, stdlib.h, string.h, …)
│   ├── src/            ← libc implementation (calls syscall.h wrappers)
│   ├── crt/
│   │   └── crt0.asm    ← C runtime start: calls main(), then exit()
│   └── Makefile        ← produces userland/libc/libc.a
├── shell/              ← userland shell (NOT the kernel/sched/shell.cpp stub)
└── bin/                ← user binaries (init, ls, cat, echo, …)
```

## Language
- Userland is **C** (not C++) unless there is a specific reason
- Use `c11` standard: `-std=c11`
- No `-ffreestanding`; userland links against libc and crt0

## C Runtime (crt0)
- `crt0.asm` is the real entry point for every userland binary (`_start`)
- It must: set up the stack, call `main(argc, argv)`, then call `exit(return_value)`
- Link order: `crt0.o` first, then objects, then `libc.a`

```asm
; ✅ GOOD - crt0.asm skeleton
global _start
extern main
extern exit

section .text
_start:
    xor  ebp, ebp
    pop  esi          ; argc
    mov  ecx, esp     ; argv
    push ecx
    push esi
    call main
    push eax
    call exit
    hlt               ; unreachable
```

## libc Implementation Rules
- Each function in libc calls a syscall wrapper from `userland/include/syscall.h`
- Do not reimplement syscall numbers — always `#include "../../include/syscall.h"`
- `malloc`/`free` use `SYS_brk` to grow the heap; no `mmap` until that syscall exists
- `printf` writes to fd `1` via `SYS_write`; no VGA or serial access

```c
// ✅ GOOD - libc write wrapping syscall
#include "../../include/syscall.h"

ssize_t write(int fd, const void *buf, size_t count) {
    return syscall3(SYS_write, fd, (int)buf, (int)count);
}

// ❌ BAD - calling kernel internals from userland
#include "kernel/dev/serial.hpp"
dev::serial_puts("hello");   // NEVER
```

## Shell (userland/shell/)
- The kernel-space `kernel/sched/shell.cpp` is a **bootstrap stub only** — it exists to prove the scheduler works
- The real shell lives in `userland/shell/` and runs as a proper user process (fork + exec model)
- The userland shell communicates via fd 0 (stdin) and fd 1 (stdout), not direct serial calls
- Shell builtins: `cd`, `echo`, `exit`, `help` at minimum

## User Binaries (userland/bin/)
- `init.c` is PID 1: it mounts filesystems and `exec`s the shell
- Every binary ends with `exit(0)` or `exit(status)` — never falls off the end of `main`
- Binaries are ELF32 executables linked against `libc.a`

## Makefile for Userland Binaries
```makefile
CC      := i686-elf-gcc
CFLAGS  := -m32 -std=c11 -Wall -O2 -Iuserland/libc/include
LDFLAGS := -m32 -nostdlib
LIBS    := userland/libc/libc.a

build/ls: userland/bin/ls.c
	$(CC) $(CFLAGS) $(LDFLAGS) userland/libc/crt/crt0.o $< $(LIBS) -o $@
```

